[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a step-by-step recipe for turning the “blank page” you see above the horizon into a proper sky with atmospheric haze.  
It works with **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.4** (the first versions that ship the `sky` layer type and the `fog` property). If you are on an older version, upgrade first.

---

## 1. What you already have

```js
// terrain source (example)
map.addSource('dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://dem-api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-tiles.json?key=YOUR_KEY',
  tileSize: 512,
  maxzoom: 14
});

map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
map.setPitch(70);
```

That gives you 3-D relief, but the area above the horizon is just the page’s CSS background.

---

## 2. Add a **sky layer**

A sky layer is a special layer type that draws a full-screen sky *behind* all other layers (except the background).  
You can choose between two styles:

| `sky-type` | What you get |
|------------|--------------|
| `gradient` | Simple vertical gradient (good for a cartoon-ish look). |
| `atmosphere` | Realistic atmosphere with sun, horizon-blend, and optional haze. |

For a natural look we’ll use `atmosphere`.

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣  Add the sky layer *after* you have added your terrain source.
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'sky',
    type: 'sky',
    paint: {
      // ------------------------------
      // 2️⃣  Choose the atmosphere model
      // ------------------------------
      'sky-type': 'atmosphere',

      // Sun direction – [lon, lat] in degrees.
      // (0,0) points to the north-east horizon; tweak to get the lighting you want.
      'sky-atmosphere-sun': [0, 0],

      // Brightness of the sun (default 15). Larger → sharper shadows.
      'sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity': 15,

      // Base colour of the atmosphere (usually a light blue).
      // You can also use a CSS colour string or an rgba array.
      'sky-atmosphere-color': '#88ccee',

      // How quickly the colour fades into the horizon.
      // 0 = hard line, 1 = very soft transition.
      'sky-atmosphere-horizon-blend': 0.2
    }
  });
});
```

### Quick visual guide

| Parameter | Effect | Typical values |
|-----------|--------|----------------|
| `sky-atmosphere-sun` | Moves the “sun” around the sky dome. | `[0,0]` (east-north), `[180,0]` (west-south), etc. |
| `sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity` | Controls the contrast of the sky and the strength of shadows on terrain. | `5–30` (lower = softer, higher = harsher). |
| `sky-atmosphere-color` | Overall tint of the sky. | Light blue `#88ccee`, sunset orange `#ffb380`, night `#001d3d`. |
| `sky-atmosphere-horizon-blend` | How “fuzzy” the horizon looks. | `0.1–0.4` (higher = more haze). |

---

## 3. Add **fog** for ground-level haze

The sky layer gives you a background, but you may also want a subtle “ground-fog” that makes distant terrain fade into the horizon. MapLibre supports this via the **style-level `fog` property** (or `map.setFog()` at runtime).

```js
map.setFog({
  // Distance (in map units) over which the fog ramps up.
  // The first value is where fog starts, the second where it becomes opaque.
  range: [0.5, 12],

  // Base colour of the fog – usually matches the sky colour.
  color: 'rgba(255,255,255,0.8)',   // light, almost white

  // How strongly the fog blends into the horizon.
  // 0 = hard line, 1 = fully blended.
  "horizon-blend": 0.2
});
```

**Result:** Distant hills and mountains will gradually lose contrast and merge into the sky, giving you that classic “mountain-haze” effect.

---

## 4. Full minimal example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain + sky + fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []   // we’ll add everything programmatically
    },
    center: [13.4, 52.5], // Berlin
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true // needed for smooth sky rendering
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // ---- DEM source -------------------------------------------------
    map.addSource('dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://dem-api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-tiles.json?key=YOUR_KEY',
      tileSize: 512,
      maxzoom: 14
    });

    // ---- Enable terrain ---------------------------------------------
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });

    // ---- Sky layer --------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'sky',
      type: 'sky',
      paint: {
        'sky-type': 'atmosphere',
        'sky-atmosphere-sun': [0, 0],
        'sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity': 15,
        'sky-atmosphere-color': '#88ccee',
        'sky-atmosphere-horizon-blend': 0.2
      }
    });

    // ---- Fog (ground haze) -----------------------------------------
    map.setFog({
      range: [0.5, 12],
      color: 'rgba(255,255,255,0.8)',
      "horizon-blend": 0.2
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `YOUR_KEY` with a valid DEM provider key (MapTiler, OpenTopo, etc.).*  

Open the page, tilt the map, and you’ll see a blue-gradient sky that fades into a soft haze at the horizon.

---

## 5. Tweaking tips

| Goal | What to adjust |
|------|----------------|
| **Sunset / sunrise colours** | Change `'sky-atmosphere-color'` to a warm orange (`#ffb380`) and lower `'sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity'` to ~8. |
| **Night sky** | Use `'sky-type': 'gradient'` with `['sky-gradient', ['rgba(10,10,30,1)', 'rgba(0,0,0,0)']]` and set a dark background colour. |
| **More dramatic haze** | Increase `range[1]` (makes fog start farther away) **or** raise `'horizon-blend'` to 0.4+. |
| **Clearer distant terrain** | Reduce `range[0]` (fog starts closer) **or** lower the fog `color` opacity. |
| **Add stars** (night mode) | Use `sky-type: 'gradient'` and add a separate `background` layer with a star texture, or overlay a custom raster layer. |

---

## 6. Common pitfalls

| Symptom | Fix |
|---------|-----|
| **Sky never appears** | Make sure you are using MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.4 and that `antialias: true` is set on the map constructor (required for the sky shader). |
| **Fog looks like a solid white overlay** | Reduce the fog `color` opacity (`rgba(...,0.5)`), or lower the `range[0]` value so the fog starts farther away. |
| **Terrain disappears when you add the sky layer** | The sky layer must be **added after** you call `map.setTerrain()`. The sky is drawn *behind* all other layers, but if you add it before the terrain source is ready the renderer may skip it. |
| **Sun direction seems wrong** | `sky-atmosphere-sun` expects **[longitude, latitude]** in degrees *relative to the map centre*, not screen coordinates. Try `[0, 0]` for east-north, `[180, 0]` for west-south, etc. |
| **Performance drop on low-end devices** | Turn off `antialias` (you lose the smooth sky) or lower the terrain `exaggeration`. The sky shader is cheap, but high-resolution DEM tiles can be heavy. |

---

