## 1️⃣ Make sure you’re on a recent MapLibre version

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
```

If you already load MapLibre from a CDN or bundle, check `maplibregl.version`. Anything **2.4.0 or newer** has both `sky` and `fog` support.

---

## 2️⃣ Add the sky layer

You can choose between two sky “styles”:

| Sky type | What it looks like | When to use it |
|----------|-------------------|----------------|
| **gradient** | Simple vertical colour gradient (e.g. sunrise-to-sunset). | Fast, works everywhere, no sun positioning needed. |
| **atmosphere** | Realistic atmospheric scattering with a sun, horizon glow, and a subtle colour shift. | When you want a more 3-D-ish look (recommended for pitched terrain). |

Below is the **atmosphere** version because it gives you the classic “haze-at-the-horizon” effect automatically.

```js
// Assuming `map` is already created and terrain is enabled
map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Add the sky layer – it must be the **topmost** layer
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'sky',
    type: 'sky',
    paint: {
      // Choose "atmosphere" for a realistic sky, or "gradient" for a simple colour ramp
      'sky-type': 'atmosphere',

      // Sun position – [azimuth, altitude] in radians.
      // 0, 0 points to the north-east horizon. Adjust to match your map orientation.
      // Example: sun at 45° azimuth, 45° altitude
      'sky-atmosphere-sun': [Math.PI / 4, Math.PI / 4],

      // Sun intensity – higher = brighter, more contrast
      'sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity': 15,

      // Optional: tweak the colour of the atmosphere (default is a nice blue)
      // 'sky-atmosphere-color': '#88ccee',

      // How quickly the horizon blends into the sky (0 = hard line, 1 = very soft)
      'sky-atmosphere-horizon-blend': 0.3
    }
  });

  // 2️⃣ Add fog – this is the “haze” that makes distant terrain fade into the sky
  //    Fog works on the whole map, not just the sky layer.
  map.setFog({
    // The colour of the fog at the camera (near) – usually matches the sky colour
    "color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.0)",

    // The colour of the fog at the horizon (far) – a slightly desaturated version of the sky
    "high-color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.5)",

    // Distance (in metres) where the fog starts and ends.
    // Adjust these numbers to control how quickly terrain fades.
    // For a typical globe-scale map, 0.5–10 works well.
    "range": [0.5, 10],

    // Optional: make the fog blend more smoothly into the horizon
    "horizon-blend": 0.2
  });
});
```

### Why the order matters

* `sky` **must be the last layer** (i.e. highest `z-index`).  
  If you add any other layer after it, that layer will cover the sky again.
* `fog` is a **global effect** – you only call `map.setFog` once, and it will affect everything that is rendered *behind* the fog (terrain, buildings, etc.).

---

## 3️⃣ (Optional) Use a gradient sky instead of atmosphere

If you prefer a simple sunrise-to-sunset gradient, replace the `paint` block with:

```js
paint: {
  'sky-type': 'gradient',
  // Bottom colour (near the horizon)
  'sky-gradient': [
    'interpolate',
    ['linear'],
    ['zoom'],
    0, 'rgba(255, 200, 150, 1)',   // low zoom – warm
    5, 'rgba(135, 206, 235, 1)'    // higher zoom – sky-blue
  ],
  // Optional: how quickly the gradient fades to the top colour
  'sky-gradient-radial-center': [0, 0],
  'sky-gradient-radial-inner-radius': 0,
  'sky-gradient-radial-outer-radius': 1
}
```

You can still keep the fog call – it works with both sky types.

