## 5️⃣ Tweaking tips

| What you want to change | Property | Typical values / tips |
|------------------------|----------|-----------------------|
| **Sun moves across the sky** | `'sky-atmosphere-sun'` | `[azimuth, altitude]` in radians. 0 rad azimuth = east, π rad = west. 0 rad altitude = horizon, π/2 rad = zenith. |
| **Brighter / darker sky** | `'sky-atmosphere-sun-intensity'` | 5 – 30. Higher = more contrast, lower = dusk-like. |
| **Harder horizon line** | `'sky-atmosphere-horizon-blend'` | 0 – 1. 0 = crisp line, 1 = very soft. |
| **More fog (more haze)** | `map.setFog({ range: [near, far] })` | Decrease `far` (e.g. `[0.5, 5]`) → fog starts earlier. Increase `near` → fog starts farther away. |
| **Fog colour matching sunset** | `high-color` & `color` | Use a warm rgba, e.g. `rgba(255,180,120,0.6)`. |
| **Gradient sky** | `'sky-type': 'gradient'` + `'sky-gradient'` | Provide an interpolated colour ramp. Good for stylised maps. |
| **Remove the default background layer** | Add a `background` layer with `paint: { "background-color": "transparent" }` **before** the sky layer, or just let the sky layer cover it. | Not strictly needed; the sky layer already overwrites the background. |

---

## 6️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **The sky stays black** | Using a MapLibre version < 2.4 (no `sky` support). | Upgrade to ≥ 2.4. |
| **Fog looks like a solid white overlay** | `range` is too small (far value < near value) or `color`/`high-color` are opaque. | Use a semi-transparent colour (`rgba(...,0.x)`) and ensure `range[0] < range[1]`. |
| **Terrain disappears when you tilt** | `antialias: true` not set on map creation (terrain shading needs it). | Add `antialias: true` to the map options. |
| **Sky disappears after adding a custom style JSON** | The style JSON does not contain a `sky` layer, and you added it *before* the style finished loading.

Below is a **complete recipe** for turning the “flat page background” you see above the horizon into a proper sky with atmospheric haze in a MapLibre GL JS map that already has 3-D terrain.

---

## 1. What you need

| Feature | How it’s provided in MapLibre |
|---------|------------------------------|
| **Sky** | Built-in *sky* layer (`type: "sky"`). It draws a procedural atmosphere (or a simple gradient) that always stays behind the terrain. |
| **Haze / Fog** | Built-in *fog* object (`map.setFog()` or a `fog` entry in the style). It blends the terrain into the sky and gives you that “ground-meets-sky” glow. |
| **Background colour** | Optional `background` layer (or CSS) for the part of the canvas that is not covered by the sky (e.g., when you zoom out far enough to see the “outside” of the map). |

All three are **client-side only** – you don’t need any extra tiles or services.

> **Version note** – Sky and fog were added in MapLibre GL JS v2.0.0. If you are on an older version, upgrade first.

---

## 2. Minimal working example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain + sky + fog</title>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // A very small style – we’ll add the rest programmatically
      sources: {
        // DEM source for terrain (MapLibre ships with a sample DEM)
        "mapbox-dem": {
          type: "raster-dem",
          url: "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/terrain/tiles.json",
          tileSize: 512,
          maxzoom: 14
        },
        // Base map – you can replace this with any vector source you like
        "osm": {
          type: "vector",
          url: "https://demotiles.maplibre.org/tiles/tiles.json"
        }
      },
      layers: [
        // Simple background – will be hidden by the sky later
        {
          id: "bg",
          type: "background",
          paint: { "background-color": "#e0e0ff" }
        },
        // Base map (streets, water, etc.)
        {
          id: "osm",
          type: "fill",
          source: "osm",
          "source-layer": "landcover",
          paint: { "fill-color": "#c8c8c8" }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [13.4, 52.5], // Berlin
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 70,
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true // important for smooth terrain
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Enable 3-D terrain
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'mapbox-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add the sky layer (type: "sky")
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'sky',
      type: 'sky',
      paint: {
        // Choose the atmosphere model (the other option is "gradient")
        'sky-type': 'atmosphere',
        // Sun position – [lon, lat] in degrees. 0° = north, 90° = east.
        // You can animate this later if you want a moving sun.
        'sky-sun': [0, 45],
        // Sun intensity (0-1). 0 = night, 1 = full daylight.
        'sky-sun-intensity': 0.9,
        // Colour of the atmosphere (default works fine)
        // 'sky-atmosphere-color': '#88c0d0',
        // Optional halo colour around the sun
        // 'sky-atmosphere-halo-color': '#ffddaa'
      }
    }, 'bg'); // insert *under* the background layer so it stays behind everything

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Add fog (haze) – this blends terrain into the sky
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // The newer API: map.setFog()
    map.setFog({
      // The distance (in metres) where fog starts and ends.
      // These numbers are *relative* to the camera altitude.
      "range": [0.5, 12],
      // Fog colour – usually matches the sky colour at the horizon.
      "color": "white",
      // How strongly the fog blends with the horizon (0-1)
      "horizon-blend": 0.2
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  (Optional) Tweak the background colour
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // If you ever zoom out far enough to see the canvas outside the map,
    // you’ll still see the background layer.  Set it to a colour that
    // matches the sky for a seamless look.
    map.setPaintProperty('bg', 'background-color', '#e0f7ff');
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Step | Code | Effect |
|------|------|--------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise** | `new maplibregl.Map({ … })` | Creates the map with a tiny style that we’ll extend. |
| **2️⃣ Terrain** | `map.setTerrain({ source: 'mapbox-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 })` | Turns the DEM into 3-D terrain. |
| **3️⃣ Sky layer** | `type: "sky"` + `sky-type: "atmosphere"` | Draws a procedural sky that automatically follows the camera pitch/rotation. |
| **4️⃣ Fog** | `map.setFog({ range, color, horizon-blend })` | Adds a smooth haze that starts close to the camera and thickens toward the horizon, giving that “ground meets sky” glow. |
| **5️⃣ Background** | `background-color` | Guarantees the canvas outside the map matches the sky colour (useful for very low zoom levels). |

---

## 3. Customising the sky & fog

### 3.1 Sky types

| `sky-type` | Description |
|------------|-------------|
| `"atmosphere"` | Real-world-like atmosphere with sun, scattering, and a subtle gradient. |
| `"gradient"` | Simple two-color gradient (good for stylised maps). Use `sky-gradient` and `sky-gradient-center` to control it. |

**Example – gradient sky**

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'sky',
  type: 'sky',
  paint: {
    'sky-type': 'gradient',
    'sky-gradient': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
                     0, '#001d4a',
                     5, '#004e92',
                     10, '#87ceeb'],
    'sky-gradient-center': [0, 0],
    'sky-gradient-radius': 90
  }
});
```

### 3.2 Sun position & intensity

- **Sun azimuth** (`sky-sun[0]`): 0 ° = north, 90 ° = east, 180 ° = south, 270 ° = west.
- **Sun elevation** (`sky-sun[1]`): 0 ° = horizon, 90 ° = zenith.
- **Intensity** (`sky-sun-intensity`): 0 = night, 1 = full daylight.

You can animate the sun by updating the paint property on each frame:

```js
let az = 0;
function animateSun() {
  az = (az + 0.1) % 360;
  map.setPaintProperty('sky', 'sky-sun', [az, 45]);
  requestAnimationFrame(animateSun);
}
animateSun();
```

### 3.3 Fog parameters

| Property | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `range` | `[near, far]` – distance (in *camera-relative* units) where fog starts and becomes opaque. Smaller `near` makes fog appear close to the camera. |
| `color` | Fog colour. Usually a light version of the sky colour. |
| `horizon-blend` | How quickly the fog colour blends into the horizon (0 = hard line, 1 = fully blended). |
| `high-color` (v2.5+) | Colour of fog at high altitude (useful for “mountain-top” haze). |
| `space-color` (v2.5+) | Colour of fog when the map is in globe projection. |

**Typical “mountain haze” setup**

```js
map.setFog({
  range: [0.5, 8],
  color: "rgba(255,255,255,0.8)",
  "high-color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.6)",
  "horizon-blend": 0.3
});
```

---

## 4. Full-style JSON alternative

If you prefer to keep everything in a static style file (e.g., `style.json`), just add the sky and fog objects there:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": { … },
  "layers": [
    { "id": "background", "type": "background", "paint": { "background-color": "#e0f7ff" } },
    { "id": "terrain", "type": "fill", "source": "osm", "source-layer": "landcover", "paint": { "fill-color": "#c8c8c8" } },
    {
      "id": "sky",
      "type": "sky",
      "paint": {
        "sky-type": "atmosphere",
        "sky-sun": [0, 45],
        "sky-sun-intensity": 0.9
      }
    }
  ],
  "fog": {
    "range": [0.5, 12],
    "color": "white",
    "horizon-blend": 0.2
  }
}
```

Then initialise the map with `style: 'style.json'`. The result is identical to the programmatic version.

---

## 5. Gotchas & performance tips

| Issue | Fix / Tip |
|-------|-----------|
| **Sky disappears after a style change** | The sky layer must be **after** any background layer but **before** any raster/vector layers that you want to appear *in front* of the sky. Re-add it after `setStyle()` if you replace the whole style. |
| **Fog looks too strong / too weak** | Adjust `range` and `horizon-blend`. Remember that the numbers are *relative to the camera altitude*, not metres on the ground. |
| **Map
